1	     1	//! `deps/llhttp/http.c`: the callbacks llhttp's generated parser makes around a message's
2	     2	//! headers and its end, ported to Rust.
3	     3	//!
4	     4	//! # The hooks
5	     5	//!
6	     6	//! llhttp's generated parser, `llhttp.c`, hands three decisions to http.c, each a hook it
7	     7	//! calls with the parser at one point of every message, the same `int (parser, p, endp)`
8	     8	//! signature as api.c's dispatchers, whose `p` and `endp` the hooks never read:
9	     9	//!
10	    10	//! - `llhttp__before_headers_complete`, once the headers are read and before `on_headers_complete`
11	    11	//!   runs: it sets whether the message upgrades the connection, so that the callback can see it. It
12	    12	//!   is [`ParserMut::before_headers_complete`], which cannot fail and returns nothing, as C's `0`
13	    13	//!   reports nothing.
14	    14	//! - `llhttp__after_headers_complete`, after `on_headers_complete`: it decides how the message's
15	    15	//!   body follows its headers, one of the six codes the parser's states switch on. It only reads
16	    16	//!   the parser, so it is [`ParserRef::after_headers_complete`], whose [`Body`] is one of the five
17	    17	//!   codes the parser goes on from, and whose [`InvalidTransferEncoding`] is the sixth, a request
18	    18	//!   whose `Transfer-Encoding` does not end with `chunked`, which the parser refuses with
19	    19	//!   `HPE_INVALID_TRANSFER_ENCODING`.
20	    20	//! - `llhttp__after_message_complete`, after `on_message_complete`: it settles whether the
21	    21	//!   connection may carry another message, marks the parser safe to finish and clears the message's
22	    22	//!   flags for the next. It is [`ParserMut::after_message_complete`], whose `bool` is C's `int`,
23	    23	//!   `1` to keep the connection.
24	    24	//!
25	    25	//! # The predicates
26	    26	//!
27	    27	//! `llhttp.h` declares http.c's two others for llhttp's users, which the hooks call too:
28	    28	//! `llhttp_message_needs_eof`, whether the message's body ends only with the input, as a
29	    29	//! response with neither a `Content-Length` nor a chunked `Transfer-Encoding` does, and
30	    30	//! `llhttp_should_keep_alive`, whether another message may follow the one parsed on the same
31	    31	//! connection, as libgit2's HTTP client asks once a response's headers are read
32	    32	//! (`transports/httpclient.c`, through `httpparser.h`'s `git_http_parser_keep_alive`). They
33	    33	//! are [`ParserRef::message_needs_eof`] and [`ParserRef::should_keep_alive`].
34	    34	//!
35	    35	//! Each reads the fields the parser fills from a message's start line and headers through
36	    36	//! `llhttp.rs`'s accessors: its type, method and status code, its HTTP version, its
37	    37	//! `Content-Length`, its upgrade, and its flag words, which stay integers until
38	    38	//! `llhttp_flags` and `llhttp_lenient_flags` have types of their own, their bits the
39	    39	//! constants the raw bindings generate (`F_*`, `LENIENT_*`). The type and the method are
40	    40	//! compared as llhttp's enums, which a byte no constant names never equals, as C's `==` on
41	    41	//! the byte has it. The version is read as llhttp reads it: a message is HTTP/1.1 or later
42	    42	//! only when its major and its minor are both above zero, so `HTTP/2.0` keeps its connection
43	    43	//! only with `Connection: keep-alive`, as `HTTP/1.0` does.
44	    44	//!
45	    45	//! # The exports
46	    46	//!
47	    47	//! The C that remains calls four of these functions: `llhttp.c`'s generated parser the three
48	    48	//! hooks, which no header declares, and libgit2's HTTP client `llhttp_should_keep_alive`.
49	    49	//! They are exported under their C names, compiled under the export feature
50	    50	//! `ffi-export-deps-llhttp-http-c` until the module switches from C. `llhttp_message_needs_eof`
51	    51	//! has no C caller outside http.c, so it has no export and leaves with http.c's C.
52	    52	//!
53	    53	//! [`ParserMut::before_headers_complete`]: super::llhttp::ParserMut::before_headers_complete
54	    54	//! [`ParserMut::after_message_complete`]: super::llhttp::ParserMut::after_message_complete
55	    55	//! [`ParserRef::after_headers_complete`]: super::llhttp::ParserRef::after_headers_complete
56	    56	//! [`ParserRef::message_needs_eof`]: super::llhttp::ParserRef::message_needs_eof
57	    57	//! [`ParserRef::should_keep_alive`]: super::llhttp::ParserRef::should_keep_alive
58	    58	
