  1	//! `deps/llhttp/http.c`: the callbacks llhttp's generated parser makes around a message's
  2	//! headers and its end, ported to Rust.
  3	//!
  4	//! # The hooks
  5	//!
  6	//! llhttp's generated parser, `llhttp.c`, hands three decisions to http.c, each a hook it
  7	//! calls with the parser at one point of every message, the same `int (parser, p, endp)`
  8	//! signature as api.c's dispatchers, whose `p` and `endp` the hooks never read:
  9	//!
 10	//! - `llhttp__before_headers_complete`, once the headers are read and before `on_headers_complete`
 11	//!   runs: it sets whether the message upgrades the connection, so that the callback can see it. It
 12	//!   is [`ParserMut::before_headers_complete`], which cannot fail and returns nothing, as C's `0`
 13	//!   reports nothing.
 14	//! - `llhttp__after_headers_complete`, after `on_headers_complete`: it decides how the message's
 15	//!   body follows its headers, one of the six codes the parser's states switch on. It only reads
 16	//!   the parser, so it is [`ParserRef::after_headers_complete`], whose [`Body`] is one of the five
 17	//!   codes the parser goes on from, and whose [`InvalidTransferEncoding`] is the sixth, a request
 18	//!   whose `Transfer-Encoding` does not end with `chunked`, which the parser refuses with
 19	//!   `HPE_INVALID_TRANSFER_ENCODING`.
 20	//! - `llhttp__after_message_complete`, after `on_message_complete`: it settles whether the
 21	//!   connection may carry another message, marks the parser safe to finish and clears the message's
 22	//!   flags for the next. It is [`ParserMut::after_message_complete`], whose `bool` is C's `int`,
 23	//!   `1` to keep the connection.
 24	//!
 25	//! # The predicates
 26	//!
 27	//! `llhttp.h` declares http.c's two others for llhttp's users, which the hooks call too:
 28	//! `llhttp_message_needs_eof`, whether the message's body ends only with the input, as a
 29	//! response with neither a `Content-Length` nor a chunked `Transfer-Encoding` does, and
 30	//! `llhttp_should_keep_alive`, whether another message may follow the one parsed on the same
 31	//! connection, as libgit2's HTTP client asks once a response's headers are read
 32	//! (`transports/httpclient.c`, through `httpparser.h`'s `git_http_parser_keep_alive`). They
 33	//! are [`ParserRef::message_needs_eof`] and [`ParserRef::should_keep_alive`].
 34	//!
 35	//! Each reads the fields the parser fills from a message's start line and headers through
 36	//! `llhttp.rs`'s accessors: its type, method and status code, its HTTP version, its
 37	//! `Content-Length`, its upgrade, and its flag words, which stay integers until
 38	//! `llhttp_flags` and `llhttp_lenient_flags` have types of their own, their bits the
 39	//! constants the raw bindings generate (`F_*`, `LENIENT_*`). The type and the method are
 40	//! compared as llhttp's enums, which a byte no constant names never equals, as C's `==` on
 41	//! the byte has it. The version is read as llhttp reads it: a message is HTTP/1.1 or later
 42	//! only when its major and its minor are both above zero, so `HTTP/2.0` keeps its connection
 43	//! only with `Connection: keep-alive`, as `HTTP/1.0` does.
 44	//!
 45	//! # The exports
 46	//!
 47	//! The C that remains calls four of these functions: `llhttp.c`'s generated parser the three
 48	//! hooks, which no header declares, and libgit2's HTTP client `llhttp_should_keep_alive`.
 49	//! They are exported under their C names, compiled under the export feature
 50	//! `ffi-export-deps-llhttp-http-c` until the module switches from C. `llhttp_message_needs_eof`
 51	//! has no C caller outside http.c, so it has no export and leaves with http.c's C.
 52	//!
 53	//! [`ParserMut::before_headers_complete`]: super::llhttp::ParserMut::before_headers_complete
 54	//! [`ParserMut::after_message_complete`]: super::llhttp::ParserMut::after_message_complete
 55	//! [`ParserRef::after_headers_complete`]: super::llhttp::ParserRef::after_headers_complete
 56	//! [`ParserRef::message_needs_eof`]: super::llhttp::ParserRef::message_needs_eof
 57	//! [`ParserRef::should_keep_alive`]: super::llhttp::ParserRef::should_keep_alive
 58	
 59	// The workspace does not set `unsafe_code` yet; this module confines its unsafe code to
 60	// `ffi_export`, which takes the lint back, and holds it to that here.
 61	#![warn(unsafe_code)]
 62	
 63	use core::fmt;
 64	
 65	use super::llhttp::{Errno, Method, ParserMut, ParserRef, ParserType};
 66	use crate::sys::libgit2 as sys;
 67	
 68	/// `llhttp_finish`'s `HTTP_FINISH_SAFE`, as the parser's `finish` byte holds it: its low byte,
 69	/// which is the whole of it (`unit_priv`'s `finish_safe_is_http_finish_safe`).
 70	const FINISH_SAFE: u8 = sys::HTTP_FINISH_SAFE.to_le_bytes()[0];
 71	
 72	/// How a message's body follows its headers, as `llhttp__after_headers_complete` decides it
 73	/// for llhttp's generated parser: the code it returns, but for the one it refuses
 74	/// ([`InvalidTransferEncoding`]).
 75	#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
 76	pub enum Body {
 77	    /// No body: the message is complete, and the parser reads the next (`0`). A `100`, `102`,
 78	    /// `103`, `204` or `304` response, the response to a `HEAD` request (`F_SKIPBODY`), a
 79	    /// message of `Content-Length: 0`, and one with no length that does not need the input's
 80	    /// end.
 81	    Empty,
 82	    /// The rest of the input is in another protocol (`1`): a `101` response, or a message that
 83	    /// upgrades the connection with no body to read first, as a `CONNECT` request. The parser
 84	    /// completes the message, and pauses at the upgrade when it is one.
 85	    Upgrade,
 86	    /// A body in chunks, `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, whatever its `Content-Length` (`2`).
 87	    Chunked,
 88	    /// A body of `Content-Length` bytes, more than zero (`3`).
 89	    Length,
 90	    /// A body that ends with the input (`4`): a response with neither a length nor a chunked
 91	    /// `Transfer-Encoding`, or one whose `Transfer-Encoding` does not end with `chunked`, and
 92	    /// such a request when the parser is lenient with either.
 93	    UntilEof,
 94	}
